# Deployment settings for Learnmap.
#
#   cp deploy/deploy.env.example deploy/deploy.env     # deploy.env is gitignored
#   $EDITOR deploy/deploy.env
#   scripts/deploy-all.sh                               # deploys to every target configured below
#   scripts/deploy-all.sh aws                           # or just one: aws | pi
#
# Any setting may instead be exported from your shell (export PI_HOST=... in
# ~/.zshrc); a variable set in the environment overrides the same one here.
#
# Every value here is read by scripts/deploy-all.sh, scripts/deploy-aws.sh and
# scripts/release.sh (the Pi). Lines starting with # are comments; values with
# spaces need quotes. Nothing in this file is ever sent to the app's users.
#
# This file holds how to reach the targets (hosts, key paths, the AWS profile
# or access key). The hosted server's provider secrets (Stripe, e-mail,
# texts) are NOT here: they live in AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store and the
# instance reads them itself (scripts/aws-env.sh; docs/aws-setup.md). Keep this
# file out of git anyway: it is listed in .gitignore. The *.example file is
# safe to commit because it holds only placeholders and paths.

# =====================================================================
# Which targets scripts/deploy-all.sh deploys to when run without arguments.
# Space-separated: "pi", "aws", or "pi aws".
# =====================================================================
DEPLOY_TARGETS="pi aws"

# =====================================================================
# Self-hosted target: the Raspberry Pi / DietPi box.
#
# These four may also be exported from your shell (e.g. in ~/.zshrc) instead
# of set here: a variable set in the environment always wins over this file,
# and scripts/release.sh runs without this file at all. The lines below are
# commented out so a fresh copy of this template does not shadow your shell.
#
# PI_HOST        user@host the deploy scripts ssh to. The user needs passwordless
#                sudo (the default on Raspberry Pi OS and DietPi).
# PI_SSH_KEY     Path to the private key for that host (optional; leave empty to
#                use your ssh config / agent). A path is safe to commit; the key
#                itself must never be in the repo.
# PI_PUBLIC_URL  The public URL the app is reached at, including the path prefix.
#                Used for the health check.
# PI_BASE_PATH   The URL prefix nginx mounts the app under ("/spanish"). Must
#                match the path in PI_PUBLIC_URL.
#
# What the Pi deploy does: cross-compiles for the Pi's CPU, rsyncs the binary
# and data/ to /opt/spanish-learning, installs the systemd unit and the nginx
# snippets (see deploy/*.conf; NGINX_TLS_VHOST="" skips nginx), writes the
# public URL to /etc/spanish-learning/env, restarts, checks /healthz.
# =====================================================================
#PI_HOST=dietpi@pi.local
#PI_SSH_KEY=~/.ssh/id_ed25519
#PI_PUBLIC_URL=https://pi.example.org/spanish
#PI_BASE_PATH=/spanish

# =====================================================================
# Cloud target: AWS. One tiny ARM instance (t4g.nano) behind Caddy, which gets
# an HTTPS certificate automatically.
#
# Cost (us-east-1, on demand, 2026): t4g.nano ~$3.07/month + 8 GB gp3 disk
# ~$0.64 + public IPv4 ~$3.65  =  about $7.40/month. New accounts come with
# free credits (or, for accounts created before July 2025, a 12-month free
# tier that covers the instance and the IPv4 address). A 1-year savings plan
# on the instance brings the steady state to ~$5/month. The stack has nothing
# else that costs money.
#
# Step-by-step account setup: docs/aws-setup.md (also served at /docs/aws-setup).
# One-time setup, in short:
# 1. Install the AWS CLI:  brew install awscli   (https://aws.amazon.com/cli/)
# 2. In the AWS console create an IAM user for deployments with the policies
#    listed in docs/aws-setup.md step 3 (EC2, CloudFormation, the instance
#    role, Parameter Store). Create an access key for it and paste it below.
#    Alternatively leave the key fields empty and set AWS_PROFILE to a profile
#    from `aws configure`.
# 3. Pick a region close to you (eu-west-1, us-east-1, ...).
# 4. SSH key: the public half of AWS_SSH_KEY is placed on the instance at
#    creation; the deploy scripts ssh in with it as ec2-user.
# 5. Provider settings: scripts/aws-env.sh init, then set each one (step 8 of
#    docs/aws-setup.md). The hosted server refuses to start until e-mail,
#    texts, Google and Stripe are all set; deploy-aws.sh checks first.
#
# AWS_DOMAIN (optional): a hostname you own, e.g. learn.example.com. After the
# first deploy, point an A record at the printed IP (and AAAA at nothing; the
# instance is IPv4). Caddy obtains the certificate as soon as DNS resolves.
# Leave empty to use the free <ip-with-dashes>.sslip.io name, which needs no
# DNS setup at all.
#
# What the AWS deploy does: creates/updates the CloudFormation stack in
# deploy/aws/cloudformation.yaml (security group, instance with a role that may
# read AWS_SSM_PATH, static IP, Caddy installed at first boot), rsyncs the
# binary and data/, writes /etc/learnmap/env (public URL + the Parameter Store
# path; no secrets), installs the unit, restarts, checks https://<host>/healthz.
# At every start the instance reads its provider settings from Parameter Store
# (deploy/aws/learnmap-env.sh) into tmpfs. Re-running is safe; the database on the
# instance is never touched. The app listens on / (no path prefix).
# To tear everything down:  scripts/deploy-aws.sh --destroy  (deletes the
# instance and its disk, i.e. the database; make a backup first).
# =====================================================================
AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
AWS_PROFILE=
AWS_REGION=eu-west-1
AWS_SSH_KEY=~/.ssh/id_ed25519
AWS_DOMAIN=
# Advanced (defaults are fine):
AWS_STACK_NAME=learnmap
AWS_INSTANCE_TYPE=t4g.nano
# Parameter Store path of the provider settings (default /skrongle/<AWS_STACK_NAME>).
AWS_SSM_PATH=
# CIDR allowed to ssh to the instance. "auto" = your current public IP, refreshed on every deploy.
AWS_ADMIN_CIDR=auto
# The instance's ssh host key is pinned on first contact in deploy/aws/known_hosts.<instance-id>,
# read from its boot console through the AWS API. If the console shows neither the key nor its fingerprint, set its ed25519
# fingerprint here (SHA256:…; on the instance: ssh-keygen -lf /etc/ssh/ssh_host_ed25519_key.pub).
AWS_SSH_HOST_KEY_FINGERPRINT=
